WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.535
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.535 --> 00:00:06.450
learn how JavaFX windows, scenes, labels, and

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:09.106
layouts work together to build and test a

00:00:09.106 --> 00:00:10.402
graphical interface.

00:00:10.402 --> 00:00:13.382
Scenes connect a window to its content; labels

00:00:13.382 --> 00:00:15.649
present text, and layouts place it.

00:00:16.298 --> 00:00:19.013
These connections help graphical applications

00:00:19.013 --> 00:00:21.668
keep window titles, content, and arrangement

00:00:21.668 --> 00:00:22.211
separate.

00:00:22.211 --> 00:00:24.927
That makes changes easier to reason about and

00:00:24.927 --> 00:00:27.642
gives you better checks when a program prints

00:00:27.642 --> 00:00:30.539
the right values but does not show the interface

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:31.323
a user needs.

00:00:31.977 --> 00:00:34.735
A campus course desk needs a small welcome

00:00:34.735 --> 00:00:35.260
display.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.215
We will first build a desk status window with

00:00:38.215 --> 00:00:39.593
one centered message.

00:00:39.593 --> 00:00:42.417
Then we'll adapt the same structure for the

00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:43.927
course welcome display.

00:00:44.573 --> 00:00:46.929
We'll inspect each actual window on the

00:00:46.929 --> 00:00:49.527
Workspace Desktop and compare its title and

00:00:49.527 --> 00:00:51.037
message with the console.

00:00:51.037 --> 00:00:53.816
We'll also close the course window and run the

00:00:53.816 --> 00:00:55.205
complete program again.

00:00:55.205 --> 00:00:57.984
A printed report and a visible, working window

00:00:57.984 --> 00:01:00.098
answer different testing questions.

00:01:00.764 --> 00:01:03.560
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:04.211
Main.java.

00:01:04.211 --> 00:01:07.332
We'll use a supplied JavaFX runner and place our

00:01:07.332 --> 00:01:10.259
window-building instructions inside its start

00:01:10.259 --> 00:01:10.714
method.

00:01:14.588 --> 00:01:17.467
A standalone graphical program needs JavaFX to

00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:20.471
start its toolkit and call the interface code on

00:01:20.471 --> 00:01:23.475
the JavaFX Application Thread, the sequence that

00:01:23.475 --> 00:01:24.915
handles interface work.

00:01:24.915 --> 00:01:27.606
The Application class provides that runner.

00:01:27.606 --> 00:01:30.547
Our Main class extends it, and its start method

00:01:30.547 --> 00:01:32.988
receives the primary Stage from JavaFX.

00:01:52.682 --> 00:01:55.062
The imports make the JavaFX class names

00:01:55.062 --> 00:01:55.673
available.

00:01:55.673 --> 00:01:58.541
Override marks start as the operation our class

00:01:58.541 --> 00:02:00.921
supplies in place of the inherited one.

00:02:00.921 --> 00:02:03.485
JavaFX calls it when the toolkit is ready.

00:02:03.485 --> 00:02:06.414
In the notebook, the supplied Fx support handles

00:02:06.414 --> 00:02:09.283
this setup differently; we'll connect those two

00:02:09.283 --> 00:02:11.907
execution paths after observing the window.

00:02:11.923 --> 00:02:15.088
A window object alone has no message to display.

00:02:15.088 --> 00:02:18.055
We'll refer to the supplied primary window as

00:02:18.055 --> 00:02:21.154
stage, create a Label containing Open, and give

00:02:21.154 --> 00:02:22.868
that label to a StackPane.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.110
A Label presents text for reading.

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.011
StackPane is a layout container that centers

00:02:28.011 --> 00:02:29.198
this single child.

00:02:39.703 --> 00:02:42.507
The label and layout are nodes, objects in the

00:02:42.507 --> 00:02:43.787
scene's content tree.

00:02:43.787 --> 00:02:46.530
The layout is the parent and the label is its

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:46.896
child.

00:02:46.896 --> 00:02:49.761
We'll use that layout as the root, the starting

00:02:49.761 --> 00:02:50.980
node for this scene.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:53.540
The Stage itself is the surrounding native

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.978
window, not a node in that content tree.

00:02:55.994 --> 00:02:58.855
The content now has a root, but it still needs a

00:02:58.855 --> 00:03:00.345
connection to the window.

00:03:00.345 --> 00:03:03.205
We'll create a Scene using that root and request

00:03:03.205 --> 00:03:05.887
a three hundred by one hundred eighty content

00:03:05.887 --> 00:03:06.185
area.

00:03:06.185 --> 00:03:08.867
Then we'll set the Stage title to Desk Status

00:03:08.867 --> 00:03:10.119
and attach the Scene.

00:03:20.174 --> 00:03:23.068
The scene holds the root, while the stage holds

00:03:23.068 --> 00:03:23.684
the scene.

00:03:23.684 --> 00:03:26.516
Its title is a separate value from the label's

00:03:26.516 --> 00:03:26.824
text.

00:03:26.824 --> 00:03:29.164
These steps configure the display, but

00:03:29.164 --> 00:03:31.811
construction and attachment do not make the

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:32.735
window visible.

00:03:32.735 --> 00:03:34.274
We still need to show it.

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:37.078
We'll call show on the stage, then print its

00:03:37.078 --> 00:03:38.783
title and the label's text.

00:03:38.783 --> 00:03:41.435
Both getters run here inside start, on the

00:03:41.435 --> 00:03:42.509
interface thread.

00:03:42.509 --> 00:03:45.098
Those lines report configured values; the

00:03:45.098 --> 00:03:47.940
Desktop will provide our separate view of the

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:48.824
actual window.

00:04:01.639 --> 00:04:03.831
We'll finish the supplied runner now.

00:04:03.831 --> 00:04:06.557
Main is the class containing this program, and

00:04:06.557 --> 00:04:08.867
main is the method where its standalone

00:04:08.867 --> 00:04:09.875
execution begins.

00:04:09.875 --> 00:04:12.719
The launch call starts JavaFX so it can call the

00:04:12.719 --> 00:04:14.733
start method we've just completed.

00:04:14.733 --> 00:04:17.281
You do not need to build this runner in the

00:04:17.281 --> 00:04:17.814
notebook.

00:04:25.737 --> 00:04:26.716
Let's click Run.

00:04:26.716 --> 00:04:29.407
The stage title is Desk Status and the label

00:04:29.407 --> 00:04:32.037
stores Open, so the print calls will report

00:04:32.037 --> 00:04:33.321
those two properties.

00:04:33.321 --> 00:04:36.257
StackPane will place its one label in the center

00:04:36.257 --> 00:04:37.052
of the scene.

00:04:37.052 --> 00:04:39.805
After the report appears, we'll switch to the

00:04:39.805 --> 00:04:41.762
Desktop to inspect that display.

00:04:50.309 --> 00:04:53.224
Desk Status appears in the native title bar, and

00:04:53.224 --> 00:04:55.471
Open is centered in the content area.

00:04:55.471 --> 00:04:58.204
The window's border and title bar sit outside

00:04:58.204 --> 00:05:00.086
the requested scene dimensions.

00:05:00.086 --> 00:05:02.819
We have now seen the result of connecting the

00:05:02.819 --> 00:05:05.673
layout to the scene, attaching the scene to the

00:05:05.673 --> 00:05:07.009
stage, and showing it.

00:05:07.147 --> 00:05:09.508
Let's close this desk status window.

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:12.393
In this standalone program, closing its last

00:05:12.393 --> 00:05:14.754
window lets the Java process finish.

00:05:14.754 --> 00:05:17.508
We'll return to the code to adapt the same

00:05:17.508 --> 00:05:19.672
structure for the course display.

00:05:23.094 --> 00:05:25.331
We'll keep the runner and the object

00:05:25.331 --> 00:05:26.076
connections.

00:05:26.076 --> 00:05:29.058
Only the Label text, scene dimensions, and Stage

00:05:29.058 --> 00:05:30.176
title will change.

00:05:30.176 --> 00:05:32.848
The new label input is Welcome to C S C two

00:05:32.848 --> 00:05:35.582
thirty-nine, the scene requests four hundred

00:05:35.582 --> 00:05:38.253
twenty by two hundred twenty, and the title

00:05:38.253 --> 00:05:39.744
input is Course Welcome.

00:05:57.239 --> 00:05:59.773
Before we run this version, predict the two

00:05:59.773 --> 00:06:00.598
console lines.

00:06:00.598 --> 00:06:03.133
Which value comes from the Stage, and which

00:06:03.133 --> 00:06:04.370
comes from the Label?

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:07.140
Also predict where the label will appear in the

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:07.906
larger scene.

00:06:07.906 --> 00:06:10.676
Pause here and trace the object connections and

00:06:10.676 --> 00:06:11.914
the two getter calls.

00:06:19.983 --> 00:06:23.027
Let's click Run and compare your prediction with

00:06:23.027 --> 00:06:25.184
the actual course welcome display.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.847
We'll read the report and then inspect the

00:06:27.847 --> 00:06:29.433
native title and content.

00:06:37.477 --> 00:06:39.578
The window title is Course Welcome.

00:06:39.578 --> 00:06:42.218
Its centered label says Welcome to C S C two

00:06:42.218 --> 00:06:42.938
thirty-nine.

00:06:42.938 --> 00:06:45.519
The console reports Window with the stage's

00:06:45.519 --> 00:06:48.040
title, then Message with the label's text.

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.440
Those values belong to separate objects.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.021
The larger Scene gives the layout more room

00:06:53.021 --> 00:06:55.602
without changing its single child's default

00:06:55.602 --> 00:06:56.202
alignment.

00:06:56.268 --> 00:06:59.151
We'll close this course window and confirm that

00:06:59.151 --> 00:07:00.010
it disappears.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:02.832
Once this standalone process finishes, running

00:07:02.832 --> 00:07:05.470
the complete program again will start a new

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:07.740
process and construct another window.

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.623
That checks more than merely repeating the same

00:07:10.623 --> 00:07:11.543
printed values.

00:07:15.117 --> 00:07:16.712
The source is unchanged.

00:07:16.712 --> 00:07:19.702
Let's click Run again, then inspect the newly

00:07:19.702 --> 00:07:22.692
created Course Welcome window on the Desktop.

00:07:30.793 --> 00:07:33.839
The new window has the same title and centered

00:07:33.839 --> 00:07:34.369
message.

00:07:34.369 --> 00:07:37.415
This run constructed a new set of objects with

00:07:37.415 --> 00:07:38.474
the same inputs.

00:07:38.474 --> 00:07:41.322
It did not revive the earlier Stage object.

00:07:41.322 --> 00:07:44.368
We'll close this final window after the check.

00:07:47.819 --> 00:07:49.888
Notebook cells already run inside a Java

00:07:49.888 --> 00:07:50.302
process.

00:07:50.302 --> 00:07:52.579
Their supplied Fx support starts the toolkit

00:07:52.579 --> 00:07:54.751
once and keeps it available after a window

00:07:54.751 --> 00:07:55.114
closes.

00:07:55.114 --> 00:07:57.390
Fx.run gives a short interface action to the

00:07:57.390 --> 00:07:59.770
JavaFX Application Thread, and Fx.closeWindows

00:07:59.770 --> 00:08:01.736
clears earlier windows in that kernel.

00:08:01.736 --> 00:08:04.012
Rerunning a complete notebook example builds

00:08:04.012 --> 00:08:06.288
fresh objects while using that same toolkit.

00:08:06.288 --> 00:08:08.720
Keep launch in this standalone runner, not in a

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:09.444
notebook cell.

00:08:09.497 --> 00:08:11.823
You connected a native Stage to a Scene, used a

00:08:11.823 --> 00:08:14.051
StackPane root to center a Label, and checked

00:08:14.051 --> 00:08:16.129
both the configured values and the visible

00:08:16.129 --> 00:08:16.476
window.

00:08:16.476 --> 00:08:18.852
Separating these jobs makes the interface easier

00:08:18.852 --> 00:08:19.792
to change and test.

00:08:19.792 --> 00:08:21.871
For a campus tutoring desk, what would you

00:08:21.871 --> 00:08:23.999
change to give the window a new title and a

00:08:23.999 --> 00:08:24.890
different message?

00:08:24.890 --> 00:08:27.217
Which checks would you make in the console, and

00:08:27.217 --> 00:08:29.147
which would require seeing the Desktop?
